1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,349
In part 1,
containers only left files in a bucket.

2
00:00:03,669 --> 00:00:08,116
Port toy makes two containers talk directly,
and needs only Docker:

3
00:00:08,116 --> 00:00:12,496
a gateway serves a web page
and forwards your text to a processor,

4
00:00:12,496 --> 00:00:14,421
which turns it into capitals.

5
00:00:14,667 --> 00:00:17,827
Think of each container
as its own little computer,

6
00:00:17,827 --> 00:00:21,110
with its own localhost
and its own full set of ports.

7
00:00:21,430 --> 00:00:25,739
Two containers can both use
port 9100 without any clash.

8
00:00:26,059 --> 00:00:28,999
The lecture calls this a network namespace:

9
00:00:28,999 --> 00:00:32,075
each container is isolated
until you wire it.

10
00:00:32,317 --> 00:00:35,935
A network you create joins them
like a small home network,

11
00:00:35,935 --> 00:00:39,677
with a phone book
that turns container names into addresses.

12
00:00:39,997 --> 00:00:42,109
Docker's default network has none.

13
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:46,702
Prerequisites: docker run hello-world checks
that Docker is running.

14
00:00:47,022 --> 00:00:50,307
It pulls a tiny image and prints
Hello from Docker!

15
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:52,790
Step 1 sets the app up by hand.

16
00:00:53,110 --> 00:00:55,834
From your workspace folder, clone the code:

17
00:00:55,834 --> 00:00:59,382
the README says clone or download
but prints no command.

18
00:00:59,702 --> 00:01:01,430
Then cd into port toy.

19
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:03,009
Pause and run them.

20
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:06,685
Two extra checks:
the files match the README's tree,

21
00:01:06,685 --> 00:01:08,427
a gateway and a processor,

22
00:01:08,427 --> 00:01:11,442
and git log shows
the commit this video pins.

23
00:01:11,683 --> 00:01:14,094
Next, docker network create toynet.

24
00:01:14,414 --> 00:01:16,590
Docker prints the new network's ID:

25
00:01:16,590 --> 00:01:19,513
that's the rail both containers
will plug into.

26
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:23,719
From the README's handy commands,
docker network ls lists it,

27
00:01:23,719 --> 00:01:26,321
beside Docker's three built-in networks.

28
00:01:26,641 --> 00:01:28,219
Pause, and create yours.

29
00:01:29,267 --> 00:01:30,974
Next, build two images.

30
00:01:31,294 --> 00:01:34,741
The two Dockerfiles are almost identical:

31
00:01:34,741 --> 00:01:38,526
a slim Python 3.14 image,
plus curl from apt.

32
00:01:38,846 --> 00:01:43,752
Each copies its code and runs it with -u,
so prints reach the logs at once.

33
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,797
The processor builds first,
and its apt layer takes a few seconds.

34
00:01:48,117 --> 00:01:51,318
Your first build also pulls
the Python base image.

35
00:01:51,567 --> 00:01:52,691
Then the gateway:

36
00:01:52,691 --> 00:01:56,329
Docker reuses the processor's apt layer
from the cache,

37
00:01:56,329 --> 00:01:59,460
because the two files match up
to the copy line.

38
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:01,380
It's done in under a second.

39
00:02:02,367 --> 00:02:08,148
Then start the processor on toynet,
with PORT set to 9200, and no -p.

40
00:02:08,468 --> 00:02:14,335
It listens on 9200 inside its own box,
but nothing on your laptop leads to it.

41
00:02:14,584 --> 00:02:15,992
Then start the gateway.

42
00:02:16,312 --> 00:02:17,749
-p publishes a port:

43
00:02:17,749 --> 00:02:23,501
it opens a street door on your laptop
that leads to a room inside the container.

44
00:02:23,821 --> 00:02:29,261
The order is IP address,
host port, container port:

45
00:02:29,261 --> 00:02:34,890
127.0.0.1,
meaning this laptop only, then door 8090,

46
00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:36,514
then room 9100.

47
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,781
The two -e flags tell the gateway
where to call:

48
00:02:41,781 --> 00:02:44,144
the name processor, port 9200.

49
00:02:44,464 --> 00:02:47,150
Lines 13 and 14 of its code read them;

50
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:50,331
the values in quotes there
are only defaults.

51
00:02:50,567 --> 00:02:52,551
Now check docker logs for each.

52
00:02:52,871 --> 00:02:59,634
The processor listens on 9200,
internal only; the gateway listens on 9100.

53
00:02:59,954 --> 00:03:01,383
Pause and start both.

54
00:03:03,617 --> 00:03:07,493
To finish step 1,
open localhost:8090 in your browser,

55
00:03:07,493 --> 00:03:09,718
type a message, and click Send.

56
00:03:10,038 --> 00:03:13,584
The reply comes back in capitals,
with three port numbers:

57
00:03:13,584 --> 00:03:17,131
where the gateway sent from,
where the processor listened,

58
00:03:17,131 --> 00:03:19,638
and where it saw the message arrive from.

59
00:03:19,884 --> 00:03:21,938
We recorded without a browser,

60
00:03:21,938 --> 00:03:25,020
so curl sent the same request
the page sends;

61
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:26,710
this is the reply it got.

62
00:03:27,434 --> 00:03:31,231
The processor listened on 9200,
the destination.

63
00:03:31,551 --> 00:03:35,882
But every connection has two ends,
so the gateway's side needs a port too.

64
00:03:36,202 --> 00:03:40,825
Its operating system picked
a temporary one, 55620,

65
00:03:40,825 --> 00:03:43,455
and stapled it to the packet.

66
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:46,105
The processor saw the same number arrive,

67
00:03:46,105 --> 00:03:48,863
because nothing
rewrites it inside the network.

68
00:03:49,183 --> 00:03:55,946
Line 38 of the gateway reads its own port;
line 32 of the processor reads the sender's.

69
00:03:56,184 --> 00:04:01,112
Send again, and the number changes:
a new connection, a new temporary port.

70
00:04:01,432 --> 00:04:02,690
Yours will differ.

71
00:04:02,934 --> 00:04:04,162
One more detail:

72
00:04:04,162 --> 00:04:08,843
the page's diagram shows 8090
by reading its own address bar.

73
00:04:09,163 --> 00:04:12,513
The container can't know
which door on your laptop you gave it.

74
00:04:12,833 --> 00:04:15,584
Pause, send a message,
and leave both running.

75
00:04:16,633 --> 00:04:18,318
Step 2 checks the ports.

76
00:04:18,638 --> 00:04:25,388
docker ps shows the gateway's mapping,
host side first:

77
00:04:25,388 --> 00:04:29,070
127.0.0.1, port 8090, to 9100.

78
00:04:29,390 --> 00:04:33,934
The processor's PORTS column is empty:
listening, but not published.

79
00:04:34,183 --> 00:04:38,556
An extra docker ps with three columns
matches the README's sample.

80
00:04:39,950 --> 00:04:43,705
What does the 127.0.0.1 do?

81
00:04:44,025 --> 00:04:48,633
This extra run leaves it out,
asking for door 8090 on every address.

82
00:04:48,953 --> 00:04:53,881
Docker refuses, port is already allocated:
our gateway holds 8090.

83
00:04:54,201 --> 00:04:57,038
The container was still created,
so remove it.

84
00:04:57,283 --> 00:05:02,026
With door 8091 it works,
and the mapping shows 0.0.0.0:

85
00:05:02,026 --> 00:05:07,459
every address, so other machines
on your network could connect.

86
00:05:07,779 --> 00:05:08,779
Remove it.

87
00:05:09,059 --> 00:05:15,310
That's why the README writes 127.0.0.1:
this laptop only.

88
00:05:15,550 --> 00:05:19,667
From the laptop,
curl on 8090 returns the gateway's page.

89
00:05:19,987 --> 00:05:24,874
curl on 9200 can't connect:
your laptop has no door to it,

90
00:05:24,874 --> 00:05:28,328
unless something else of yours uses 9200.

91
00:05:28,566 --> 00:05:32,158
From inside the gateway,
docker exec runs curl,

92
00:05:32,158 --> 00:05:35,521
asking for the name processor, on port 9200.

93
00:05:35,841 --> 00:05:37,356
The answer: ok.

94
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:38,600
Ponder this.

95
00:05:38,901 --> 00:05:42,208
The same command
with localhost instead of processor.

96
00:05:42,528 --> 00:05:43,552
What answers?

97
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:51,400
Nothing.

98
00:05:51,531 --> 00:05:55,556
Inside the gateway,
localhost is the gateway itself,

99
00:05:55,556 --> 00:05:58,187
and nothing there listens on 9200.

100
00:05:58,507 --> 00:06:02,539
That's the second puzzle:
each container has its own localhost.

101
00:06:02,859 --> 00:06:08,405
An extra check: on 9100, its own port,
localhost returns the page.

102
00:06:08,650 --> 00:06:10,207
So how did the name work?

103
00:06:10,527 --> 00:06:12,049
From the handy commands,

104
00:06:12,049 --> 00:06:15,791
docker network inspect shows an address
for each container,

105
00:06:15,791 --> 00:06:18,548
from toynet's own address range, its subnet.

106
00:06:19,650 --> 00:06:24,106
Each container asks
Docker's built-in phone book,

107
00:06:24,106 --> 00:06:29,015
at 127.0.0.11,
which turns names into those addresses.

108
00:06:29,335 --> 00:06:33,026
Addresses can change
when a container restarts; names stay.

109
00:06:33,266 --> 00:06:35,147
Another handy command,

110
00:06:35,147 --> 00:06:39,337
docker exec -it gateway sh,
opens a shell inside;

111
00:06:39,337 --> 00:06:42,119
its hostname is the container ID.

112
00:06:42,439 --> 00:06:47,666
One aside: the processor image has curl too,
though the README implies it doesn't.

113
00:06:47,986 --> 00:06:50,034
Pause, and test from both sides.

114
00:06:51,683 --> 00:06:53,603
Step 3 changes the ports.

115
00:06:53,923 --> 00:07:02,840
First the street door: remove the gateway,
and start it again on 127.0.0.1, port 8095.

116
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:05,763
The room inside stays 9100.

117
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,283
The page works on 8095.

118
00:07:08,603 --> 00:07:14,235
Two extra checks: 8090 no longer connects,
and docker ps shows the new mapping.

119
00:07:14,555 --> 00:07:16,560
Pause and move your gateway's door.

120
00:07:19,050 --> 00:07:20,053
Why do this?

121
00:07:20,373 --> 00:07:23,957
Another app on your laptop
might already hold 8090.

122
00:07:24,277 --> 00:07:29,311
Doors on the laptop must be unique;
rooms inside containers never collide.

123
00:07:29,550 --> 00:07:34,393
A small slip: the gateway's log
still says open localhost:8090.

124
00:07:34,713 --> 00:07:37,294
That text is hard-coded in line 53.

125
00:07:37,614 --> 00:07:40,216
Trust docker ps and the page instead.

126
00:07:40,466 --> 00:07:46,098
Next, the processor's port: remove it,
and start it with PORT set to 9300.

127
00:07:46,418 --> 00:07:49,554
The README says:
send a message, and it should fail.

128
00:07:49,950 --> 00:07:51,443
It does, but quietly.

129
00:07:51,763 --> 00:07:55,625
The page has no error handling,
so nothing on it changes;

130
00:07:55,625 --> 00:08:00,232
the captured request got only curl's error,
Empty reply from server.

131
00:08:00,466 --> 00:08:04,005
An extra docker logs
on the gateway shows why:

132
00:08:04,005 --> 00:08:06,390
it found the processor by name,

133
00:08:06,390 --> 00:08:11,175
but the connection was refused:
nobody listens on 9200 anymore.

134
00:08:11,495 --> 00:08:14,909
A wrong name fails differently:
see the exercises.

135
00:08:15,150 --> 00:08:16,150
Ponder this.

136
00:08:16,451 --> 00:08:20,270
Which one setting fixes it,
and does any image need rebuilding?

137
00:08:27,116 --> 00:08:28,908
The gateway's processor port.

138
00:08:29,228 --> 00:08:35,607
Remove the gateway and start it with 9300,
back on 8090, and the message goes through.

139
00:08:35,927 --> 00:08:39,361
No image was rebuilt:
it's only configuration.

140
00:08:39,681 --> 00:08:41,132
Pause and fix yours.

141
00:08:42,116 --> 00:08:43,850
The two settings must agree:

142
00:08:43,850 --> 00:08:47,194
the processor's PORT
and the gateway's processor port.

143
00:08:47,433 --> 00:08:52,169
Finally, clean up before Compose:
remove both containers, then the network.

144
00:08:52,489 --> 00:08:56,244
An extra check shows only
hello-world's old container left.

145
00:08:56,564 --> 00:08:59,401
Skip this, and Compose hits a name conflict.

146
00:08:59,721 --> 00:09:01,022
Pause and clean up.

147
00:09:04,317 --> 00:09:09,544
Step 4: Docker Compose, which builds
and starts the whole app from one YAML file.

148
00:09:09,864 --> 00:09:14,344
The README shows an excerpt;
open the whole docker-compose.yml.

149
00:09:14,664 --> 00:09:18,034
Almost every flag you typed
by hand has a home in it.

150
00:09:18,284 --> 00:09:22,871
--name becomes container name,
and the build command becomes build.

151
00:09:23,191 --> 00:09:26,223
-p becomes ports, -e becomes environment,

152
00:09:26,223 --> 00:09:29,996
and the network is named
at the bottom of the file.

153
00:09:31,884 --> 00:09:33,038
Two keys are new:

154
00:09:33,038 --> 00:09:36,706
the volumes key mounts the gateway's folder
into /app,

155
00:09:36,706 --> 00:09:40,055
and the depends on key
starts the processor first.

156
00:09:40,375 --> 00:09:44,449
All of step 1 collapses into one command:
docker compose up.

157
00:09:44,684 --> 00:09:45,684
Run it.

158
00:09:45,836 --> 00:09:49,127
Compose builds both images,
all from the cache,

159
00:09:49,127 --> 00:09:53,750
and names them after the folder
and the service: port_toy-gateway.

160
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,922
Then it creates the network
and both containers,

161
00:09:56,922 --> 00:10:00,635
and prints both banners,
each line prefixed with its service.

162
00:10:00,955 --> 00:10:02,107
Pause and run it.

163
00:10:02,350 --> 00:10:04,163
Open the page: it works.

164
00:10:04,483 --> 00:10:07,608
In a second terminal,
cd into port toy first:

165
00:10:07,608 --> 00:10:10,734
Compose reads the file
in the current folder.

166
00:10:11,054 --> 00:10:14,190
There, docker compose ps
shows the same mapping.

167
00:10:14,434 --> 00:10:18,359
An extra network ls shows
that Compose made toynet,

168
00:10:18,359 --> 00:10:22,284
and compose exec reaches the processor
by name: ok.

169
00:10:22,650 --> 00:10:27,194
The README's table pairs each by-hand
command with its Compose version.

170
00:10:27,514 --> 00:10:30,821
In the first terminal,
your requests now show in the log.

171
00:10:31,067 --> 00:10:34,188
The README stops the app
at the end of step 4;

172
00:10:34,188 --> 00:10:37,446
we stop it now, in the foreground:
press Ctrl+C.

173
00:10:37,766 --> 00:10:41,926
Compose says it's stopping gracefully,
but it takes about 20 seconds.

174
00:10:45,134 --> 00:10:47,720
Python, each container's first process,

175
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:51,897
ignores the polite stop signal
unless it's written to catch it.

176
00:10:52,217 --> 00:10:55,758
So Docker waits 10 seconds per container,
then kills it.

177
00:10:58,667 --> 00:11:01,462
Exit code 137 means killed.

178
00:11:01,782 --> 00:11:03,333
docker compose ps -a:

179
00:11:03,333 --> 00:11:06,880
both containers exited,
but they're still there.

180
00:11:07,117 --> 00:11:11,379
docker compose down removes the containers
and the network,

181
00:11:11,379 --> 00:11:13,474
and nothing is left in ps -a.

182
00:11:13,794 --> 00:11:16,120
Ctrl+C stops; down removes.

183
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:18,381
Pause, and run the whole cycle.

184
00:11:19,417 --> 00:11:21,151
Back to the README's order:

185
00:11:21,151 --> 00:11:25,198
up -d starts the app in the background
and returns your prompt.

186
00:11:25,434 --> 00:11:26,778
Two handy commands.

187
00:11:27,098 --> 00:11:30,698
docker compose logs -f
follows the gateway's log;

188
00:11:30,698 --> 00:11:33,050
Ctrl+C stops only the following.

189
00:11:33,370 --> 00:11:36,771
docker compose exec
gateway sh opens a shell,

190
00:11:36,771 --> 00:11:40,324
where the environment
shows processor and 9200.

191
00:11:41,967 --> 00:11:45,743
Next, the README says
to repeat the port experiments in the file.

192
00:11:46,063 --> 00:11:52,762
Change 8090 to 8095 and run up -d:
Compose recreates only the gateway.

193
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:57,331
The page answers on 8095;
change it back and apply again.

194
00:12:00,184 --> 00:12:02,531
We added a comment to the processor's code.

195
00:12:02,851 --> 00:12:06,797
Changed code that's copied
into an image needs --build.

196
00:12:07,117 --> 00:12:11,362
Only the copy layer is new,
and only the processor is recreated.

197
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:16,067
The gateway's folder is mounted,
and it rereads its page on each load:

198
00:12:16,067 --> 00:12:19,195
edits show on the next reload,
without a rebuild.

199
00:12:20,050 --> 00:12:24,615
To finish, docker compose down;
nothing is left in ps -a.

200
00:12:24,935 --> 00:12:28,451
The images remain,
sharing about 200 megabytes;

201
00:12:28,451 --> 00:12:30,397
remove them to free space.

202
00:12:30,717 --> 00:12:32,210
Port toy costs nothing.

203
00:12:32,530 --> 00:12:33,981
Pause and shut it down.

204
00:12:34,217 --> 00:12:36,990
To test yourself, pause after each question.

205
00:12:37,310 --> 00:12:40,326
One:
which files define a mega-pipeline image,

206
00:12:40,326 --> 00:12:43,604
and what does run use
when there's no local image?

207
00:12:43,850 --> 00:12:47,048
Two: which three gates must a call
to your bucket pass,

208
00:12:47,048 --> 00:12:48,735
and where does each one fail?

209
00:12:49,055 --> 00:12:52,212
Three: how does a git-ignored key
end up in an image,

210
00:12:52,212 --> 00:12:53,344
and what's the fix?

211
00:12:53,584 --> 00:13:00,539
Four: -p 127.0.0.1:8095:9100.

212
00:13:00,859 --> 00:13:03,888
Which number does the browser use,

213
00:13:03,888 --> 00:13:09,413
which does the gateway bind,
and what does 127.0.0.1 restrict?

214
00:13:09,733 --> 00:13:14,896
Five: inside the gateway, what is localhost,
and which name reaches the processor?

215
00:13:15,134 --> 00:13:20,039
Next lecture, a command-line container
calls a database container by name

216
00:13:20,039 --> 00:13:23,444
on a Compose network,
with the same secrets mount:

217
00:13:23,444 --> 00:13:25,011
parts 1 and 2 together.

218
00:13:25,331 --> 00:13:27,379
Answers are in the exercises.

